<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Pulse AI entitlements — direct distribution (Developer ID), NOT the
  Mac App Store.

  Choice by choice:
  - com.apple.security.app-sandbox = false
    Pulse's job is reading OTHER processes (proc_pidinfo), LaunchAgent
    plists in three directories, and model stores across the home
    folder. The App Sandbox makes the core product impossible, so the
    app ships unsandboxed via direct distribution — the same posture
    as iStat Menus and Stats. The risk this creates is answered with
    the controls below plus the hardened runtime (build setting
    ENABLE_HARDENED_RUNTIME = YES in the project — entitlements alone
    do not enable it).
  - com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit = false
    No JavaScript engine, no runtime code generation — nothing in
    Pulse needs JIT. Denied explicitly.
  - com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation = false
    Only code signed by the same Team ID (or Apple) may be loaded.
    Pulse has no plugins; library validation stays fully on.
  No network server/client entitlements are needed outside the
  sandbox; loopback HTTP is governed by the ATS settings in
  Resources/Info.plist (NSAllowsLocalNetworking) and by the hardcoded
  127.0.0.1 allow-list in Services/AIEngines.swift.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<false/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<false/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
